In C, you can terminate a program in several ways. Here are some common methods:


1. Using an external signal:
On Unix-based systems, you can send an external signal to a running program to terminate it. For example, you can use the kill command to send a SIGTERM signal to a program.
2. Using a system call:
You can also terminate a program by using a system call. For example, you can use the exit() system call to terminate a program.
3. Using a library function:
Some libraries provide functions to terminate a program. For example, the Qt library provides the qApp->quit() function to terminate a Qt application.
Here is an example of using a system call to terminate a program:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
int main(void) {
  printf("Hello, world!\n");
  exit(0);
}
```
The exit() system call is used to terminate the program. When the system call is made, the program is immediately terminated. All functions called after the exit() system call will not be executed.
